316 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (A 4-bit parity generator was implemented using a single assign statement with the reduction XOR operator \(^\). The output becomes 1 when the input contains an odd number of 1s and 0 otherwise.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
317 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The $countones\(\) function is a built-in SystemVerilog function that counts the number of bits set to 1 in the input data. In this task, it is used to calculate the expected parity value for comparison with the module output. The expression $countones\(data\) % 2 checks whether the number of 1s in the 4-bit input is odd or even. If the remainder is 1, the input contains an odd number of 1s, so the expected parity is 1. If the remainder is 0, the number of 1s is even, so the expected parity is 0. This provides a simple and reliable way to verify that the parity generator produces the correct output for all 16 input combinations.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The testbench checks all 16 possible input combinations. For each input, the expected parity is calculated using $countones\(data\) % 2 and compared with the module output. Every test passes, confirming the correctness of the design.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The waveform shows the 4-bit input data changing sequentially through all 16 possible binary values during the simulation. As the input changes, the parity output is updated automatically by the reduction XOR operator. The expected signal, calculated using $countones\(data\) % 2, follows the same pattern as the generated parity output. Throughout the simulation, both signals remain identical, confirming that the implementation is correct. The initial unknown \(X\) value on the expected signal occurs before it is assigned a value in the testbench and is a normal part of digital simulation. Once the simulation begins, both parity and expected produce matching outputs for every input combination, verifying the correct operation of the 4-bit parity generator.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
